---
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import Container from '../Container.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../SectionHeader.astro';

/*
  Pitch page section 3 — the vibe (docs/pitch-demo-sitemap.md §4.3, owner-required).

  NO PHOTOGRAPHY OF PEOPLE — `creative-director` review fix 3, and the sitemap's
  highest-rated integrity risk on the page. The obvious execution of "show the
  vibe" is a grid of faces or a wall of gathering photography; both read as
  evidence of a community that does not exist yet, and stock crowds read as it
  twice over. So this section is built from type, colour, space and language
  only: no <img>, no background photograph, no mood-board tiles of other
  people's work. It is not a placeholder for imagery — it is the section.

  The tonal position is stated once in the heading, and then the sharper half of
  the job is the negation list: naming what New Earth Network is not does more
  in three lines than three more adjectives. Those three lines come from the
  sitemap itself, not from this file.

  COPY STATUS (2026-09-10): the heading and lead paragraph are owner-supplied
  copy — "Livelier. Funner. Realer." and the "what Gaia does well, and how we
  differ" paragraph. The three cards below are the owner's own dictated
  wording, verbatim, one Gaia-vs-New-Earth-Network comparison per product
  line (Film, Music, Events). They replaced an earlier negation list ("Not a
  wellness catalog," etc.) that sat awkwardly under a paragraph promising
  "examples" of products — the owner chose not to bring that beat back
  elsewhere on the page.

  INTEGRITY: the Music card's claim that Gaia is "less likely to promote 432 Hz
  Electronica or Rap affirmation music" speculates about a competitor's limits
  — `copywriter` flagged this as reading insecure in a pitch (sitemap §4.3/§4.4).
  The owner reviewed and kept it as a deliberate call, not an oversight. Every
  New Earth Network clause across all three cards stays future tense: these are plans, not a
  catalog that exists. The Events card links "Garden Party" to the event's
  public Instagram profile; it does not claim an individual or partner.

  ART DIRECTION (2026-09-10) — three cards, per the site owner. Copy above is
  byte-for-byte what ships; only its presentation is designed below.

  The card surface is the site's existing card language, not a new one:
  --color-bg-elevated, --radius-md, --shadow-float — the same three values
  EventCard, CityCard and CommunityProof already use. Cards float on shadow,
  no outline (tokens.css, "Elevation"). The dashed --border-placeholder is
  deliberately NOT used: that treatment means "scaffolding awaiting real
  content," and this card carries final owner-approved copy.

  THE CATEGORY IS A HEADING, NOT A CAPTION. Each card is one paragraph of the
  owner's own prose, so the only structural decision left is what anchors it.
  A 13px tracked-out cyan label — this page's treatment for repeated-item
  metadata (PitchPlan's step numerals, PitchWho's roles) — would leave three
  cards that are each a lone grey paragraph, in the one section on the page
  whose entire argument is that New Earth Network is livelier than the
  incumbent. Set as an <h3> instead, the three categories carry a horizontal
  rhythm across the row, and the section stops contradicting its own headline.
  It is also the correct document outline: these are three subheads under the
  section's h2, not three labels.

  What carries that rhythm changed when the owner renamed the categories from
  Film / Music / Events to More Creativity / Less Corporate / Higher Vibes
  (2026-09-10). It used to be size — three big single words. It is now the
  parallel grammar: comparative + noun, three times, More / Less / Higher. That
  only reads if the three sit on one line each and their paragraphs start
  level, which is what the retuned .label sizing below protects and what the
  old 32px setting broke. Size stopped being the mechanism, so size gave way.

  SO THE CARDS SPEND NO CYAN. That is the one deliberate departure from the
  page's other repeated-item labels, and it is a quantity argument, not a
  taste one: three headings in the signal colour is a completely different
  amount of light from three 13px marks, and it would leave this section with
  four lit elements competing (the kicker plus three titles) where the rule is
  that light is earned and one thing is loudest. The kicker keeps the cyan.
  The cards are carried by the type scale and the elevated surface, which is
  what those exist for. Flagged for `creative-director` explicitly — the
  cumulative cyan budget across the whole pitch page is a page-level call, and
  this section should not be the only one that quietly answers it.

  CARDS ARE NOT LINK WRAPPERS. Garden Party is an inline source link, but the
  card itself still has no destination. A hover lift or glow on the whole card
  would promise an interaction it cannot complete. The inline link uses the
  page's established underlined cyan treatment and the global focus-visible
  state. Nothing in this section animates, so there is no reduced-motion branch.

  Three-up at 960px, one column below. Not the 720px this page uses elsewhere
  (PitchWho's roster): those cards carry one short line each, these carry a
  full paragraph, and at 720px a third of the rail is ~170px of text. 960px is
  where each card still holds a real measure (~368px of text at 1440px, about
  50 characters a line — measured, not estimated). Below it, full-width
  stacked cards; no 2-up step, which would orphan the third card.
*/

interface TextLink {
  label: string;
  href: string;
}

type TextPart = string | TextLink;

// One shape for all three, matching the copy: a category and the owner's own
// paragraph about it. Text parts allow a named source to remain a semantic link
// without injecting HTML. The explicit type keeps incomplete cards from building.
interface Example {
  label: string;
  text: TextPart[];
}

const examples: Example[] = [
  {
    label: 'More Creativity',
    text: [
      'Gaia focuses their film content on documentaries. While New Earth Network will also produce documentaries, we will differentiate by also producing movies to create worlds and tell stories that help viewers imagine, innerstand, and perceive the New Earth.',
    ],
  },
  {
    label: 'Less Corporate',
    text: [
      'While Gaia may promote meditation, sound bath, and singing bowl healing, they are less likely to promote 432 Hz Electronica or Rap affirmation music. New Earth Network will not be bound to such limitations.',
    ],
  },
  {
    label: 'Higher Vibes',
    text: [
      'Gaia produces events such as conferences with speaker panels. While New Earth Network will also produce such events, we will also throw 5D parties. A great example is the ',
      {
        label: 'Garden Party',
        href: 'https://www.instagram.com/garden.party.la/',
      },
      '.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Section tone="deep" id="vibe">
  <Container>
    <SectionHeader kicker="The vibe" heading="Livelier. Funner. Realer." />

    <p class="lead">
      What Gaia does, it does well. And while New Earth Network will operate on a similar
      model, we will differentiate ourselves significantly in energy, frequency, and vibe.
      Below are some examples of how we can offer and distribute similar types of products
      while creating a distinctly different experience.
    </p>

    <ul class="examples" role="list">
      {
        examples.map((example) => (
          <li class="card">
            <h3 class="label">{example.label}</h3>
            <p class="text">
              {example.text.map((part) =>
                typeof part === 'string' ? (
                  part
                ) : (
                  <a class="pitch-vibe-link" href={part.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                    {part.label}
                  </a>
                ),
              )}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Container>
</Section>

<style>
  .lead {
    color: var(--color-text-body);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-16);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .examples {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }

  .card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding: var(--space-6);
    background: var(--color-bg-elevated);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    /* Opaque fill + shadow, no outline: an object floating on the field should
       occlude it (tokens.css, --color-bg-veil). --color-bg-elevated is 1.19x the
       sky's median luminance, so the card reads as lifted rather than as a hole
       punched in the nebula. */
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-float);
  }

  /* The category is the card's anchor, not its metadata, so it is set as a
     heading and not as a small tracked-out accent label. This is the section
     arguing for more energy, so it should not be the quietest section on the
     page.

     RETUNED 2026-09-10 for the owner's new headings. These were single words
     (Film / Music / Events) and the treatment was built on that: --text-lg,
     inherited weight 500, three big words carrying a horizontal rhythm across
     the row. Two-word claims are a different object and broke it two ways,
     both measured in the browser rather than argued:

     1. FIT. Three-up, the card's text column is 214px at the 960px breakpoint.
        At --text-lg (30.4px there) "More Creativity" measures 217px and wrapped;
        "Less Corporate" cleared by 1.8px. So the row's narrowest state was
        already broken between 960-969px and was inside the noise floor
        everywhere below ~1100px — one card's paragraph starting a line lower
        than its neighbours', which is the misalignment tell. It also broke
        outright for anyone browsing at a larger default font size, since the
        rem half of the clamp scales while the card's vw-derived width does not.
        Single words had ~100px of slack and never exposed this.
     2. VOICE. Six words at 32px across the row out-massed the section's own h2
        (--text-xl). Something has to be loudest and it is the headline.

     So the heading is sized to the CARD, not to the viewport: --text-lg while
     the cards are stacked full-width (nothing about the new copy is under
     pressure there — 205px of ink in a 658px column at 768px), stepping down
     one rung to --text-md in the three-up rule below, where the column is
     narrow. Same place the padding already steps; no new breakpoint, no new
     token.

     Weight goes up to 600 as the size comes down — the site's h1/h2 weight,
     restoring the 500 that the global h3 rule steps down to. A claim wants to
     be dense rather than large, and it pays back at 320px, where --text-md's
     floor (20px) sits close to the 17px body and needs the weight to still
     read as a title. `balance` rather than the paragraph's `pretty` because
     these are two-word lines: if one ever does wrap it should split evenly
     rather than drop "Creativity" alone onto a second line under "More".

     One accepted degradation, measured, not assumed: at a 20px browser default
     font size the three-up row still wraps between 960px and ~1010px, because
     the type scales with rem while the column is derived from vw. It clears by
     1024px. That is reflow behaving correctly — nothing clips or overlaps, the
     wrap is even, and the only cost is that the paragraphs stop starting level
     for that band. Closing it entirely would mean either subgrid-linking the
     heading row across the three cards (which forces the inter-card gap and
     the label's own bottom margin to become the same value) or pushing the
     three-up breakpoint to 1024px and giving up a working three-up layout for
     everyone at 960-1023px. Neither is worth it; the old 32px setting wrapped
     here at every default font size, so this is the strictly better failure.

     Deliberately still NOT cyan, and the new copy strengthens that rather than
     reopening it: these read as claims now, and three lit claims plus the
     kicker is four competing lights where the rule is that one thing is
     loudest. The kicker keeps the cyan; the cards use the type system.
     Inherits face, tracking and --color-text-primary from the global h1-h4
     rule. Contrast: #f7f4ff on #422a58 = 11.35:1, unchanged — nothing here
     touches colour. */
  .label {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    text-wrap: balance;
    /* One rung more than the old --space-4. At --text-lg a 16px gap read as
       the heading sitting on its paragraph; now that the claim and the body
       are closer in size, the space is what separates claim from evidence. */
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* The paragraph keeps the page's default body size rather than stepping down
     into caption territory: it is the card's whole substance, and it is the
     same voice as the lead paragraph above the row, so it should read at the
     same size. Only the colour is set — everything else is inherited.
     Contrast: #ded9ef on #422a58 = 8.97:1. */
  .text {
    color: var(--color-text-body);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    /* Same measure as .lead above the row, so the section reads at one line
       length throughout. It only binds while the cards are stacked and full
       width — at 768px an uncapped card runs ~70 characters a line, past a
       comfortable measure. Three-up, the card is already narrower than 60ch,
       so this does nothing there and costs no breakpoint to say so. */
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  /* This link is rendered from a typed text-part inside an array map. Keep its
     uniquely named visual rule global so Astro's dev HMR cannot temporarily
     strand the anchor with a stale scope attribute when replacing this
     component's CSS; the global anchor reset would otherwise remove the only
     persistent inline-link treatment after the initial browser paint. */
  :global(.pitch-vibe-link) {
    color: var(--color-electric-cyan);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }

  @media (min-width: 960px) {
    .examples {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }

    /* Padding steps up with the card, not independently of it — same 4px grid,
       one rung. */
    .card {
      padding: var(--space-8);
    }

    /* ...and the heading steps DOWN one rung as the column narrows, for the
       fit and voice reasons in .label above. At 24px the longest claim is
       171px in a 214px column — 20% slack at the tightest point of the whole
       range, against 1.8px before — so all three sit on one line and the three
       paragraphs start on the same baseline across the row. */
    .label {
      font-size: var(--text-md);
    }
  }
</style>
